## Why This Is Asked
This question tests advanced AWS authorization patterns including ABAC with IAM tags, Lake Formation permissions, and cross-account data governance in regulated environments. It probes understanding of data catalog discipline, encryption strategies, revocation latency requirements, and external auditor access workflows.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tag-based access control (TBAC) combined with Lake Formation fine-grained grants
- Cross-account role trusts with least-privilege permission boundaries
- S3 prefix-level isolation per tenant with envelope encryption (KMS)
- Data catalog resource links and table/column-level permissions
- Audit trail generation via Athena, CloudTrail, and Lake Formation audit logs

## Solution Architecture

### Cross-Account Trust Model
```javascript
// Analytics Account Trust Policy (tenant-specific role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {
      "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::INGEST_ACCOUNT:root"
    },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
    "Condition": {
      "StringEquals": {
        "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${s3:tenant-id}"
      },
      "StringLike": {
        "s3:prefix": "data-lake/${s3:tenant-id}/*"
      }
    }
  }]
}
```

### IAM Tag-Based Permission Policy
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": [
      "s3:GetObject",
      "s3:PutObject",
      "s3:ListBucket"
    ],
    "Resource": [
      "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
      "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*"
    ],
    "Condition": {
      "StringEquals": {
        "s3:prefix": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*"
      }
    }
  }]
}
```

### Lake Formation Grant Scope
- Grant DATABASE permission on tenant-specific catalog databases
- Grant TABLE permission filtered to tenant-prefixed table patterns
- Use data catalog resource links in Analytics account pointing to Governance catalog

## Test Plan

| Test Case | Expected Result |
|-----------|-----------------|
| Tenant A reads own data | Access granted via matched tag |
| Tenant A reads Tenant B data | Access denied—prefix mismatch |
| Revoke Tenant A access | Role trust removed within 2 hours via IAM/Lake Formation API |
| External auditor cross-account read | Time-limited read-only role with Athena query access |
| Audit log verification | CloudTrail shows principal, tenant-id, timestamp, resource |

## External Auditor Sharing
Grant time-limited read-only access via a dedicated auditor role in the Governance account with Athena query permissions restricted to specific databases.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end AWS streaming design, data locality, replay semantics, and observability across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality, replay, idempotency, cross-region replication, shard throttling, and cost trade-offs.
- Durability vs latency in KDS/Kafka vs Firehose patterns.
- Observability via CloudWatch, X-Ray, and structured metrics.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent sink to DynamoDB
async function sinkEvent(event){
  const key = event.tenantId + '#' + event.id;
  await dynamo.put({
    TableName: 'TenantEvents',
    Item: { PK: key, ...event },
    ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(PK)'
  }).catch(err => {
    if (err.code === 'ConditionalCheckFailedException') return; // idempotent retry
    throw err
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle shard throttling and bursty traffic?
- How would you onboard new tenants without impacting latency or isolation?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-region, multi-account DR with strict IAM boundaries and automated failover.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-region replication (S3 CRR, DynamoDB Global Tables)
- Route 53 failover with health checks
- Per-tenant IAM roles with least privilege and explicit denies
- IaC automation and DR testing

## Code Example

```terraform
# Minimal Terraform snippet for Route53 failover
resource "aws_route53_health_check" "region_a" {
  fqdn = "api.example.com"
  type = "HTTPS"
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "primary" {
  name = "api.example.com"
  type = "A"
  zone_id = var.zone_id
  set_identifier = "RegionA"
  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "primary"
    health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.region_a.id
  }
  alias {
    name = aws_lb.region_a.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_lb.region_a.zone_id
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "secondary" {
  name = "api.example.com"
  type = "A"
  zone_id = var.zone_id
  set_identifier = "RegionB"
  failover_routing_policy {
    type = "secondary"
    health_check_id = aws_route53_health_check.region_b.id
  }
  alias {
    name = aws_lb.region_b.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_lb.region_b.zone_id
  }
}
```

